{
  "content": "### Sequencing: rebuild the task spec as the conversation evolves\n- Build the task spec **only from user messages**, and update it after every new user turn. Later user messages can revise, complete, or invalidate earlier constraints.\n- Reconcile each new user shard against the full accumulated spec, not just the latest turn. Treat the conversation as a **running state machine**: what is known, what changed, and what remains unresolved.\n- Before judging the assistant, reconstruct the **current end-to-end user objective** and compare the assistant\u2019s latest turn against that final state. Avoid stale judgments based on an earlier partial interpretation.\n- Distinguish **intermediate correctness** from **final-task correctness**. A step can be valid under an earlier shard yet still be insufficient once later user text adds the missing condition, transformation, or scope.\n- When the user provides enough later information to complete the task, stop carrying forward older uncertainty. Prefer a single reconciled spec over competing partial branches.\n\n### State and unit tracking: follow what the user has actually changed\n- When the same entity reappears later, determine whether it is the **same object in a later state** or a different subset/category. Propagate state changes instead of treating mentions independently.\n- For partial amounts, totals, leftovers, or accumulations, reconstruct the **current state first** before reasoning backward. An earlier intermediate value may become obsolete once later information arrives.\n- Track the **unit or level** of the current quantity explicitly. Many divergences come from silently switching between per-item, per-group, subtotal, and overall interpretations.\n- When a later user turn adds a distribution phrase such as \u201ceach,\u201d \u201cfor every,\u201d or \u201cper,\u201d check whether the effective quantity must now scale across multiple recipients/groups. This often turns a correct local result into an incomplete global one.\n- For rates, proportions, or time-based relations, verify the **direction of the relation** and the base quantity it applies to. Do not silently convert one kind of quantity into another.\n- When the user later provides a formula or explicit relationship, use it **literally** as the governing rule and recompute from the accumulated facts.\n\n### Diagnostic patterns: what signals a real mismatch\n- Flag any assistant step that fills a gap the user has **not actually filled**. Unsupported assumptions are errors even if they produce plausible results.\n- If the assistant keeps asking for information after the user has already provided it, mark that as **stale-analysis divergence** rather than caution.\n- If later user turns make earlier ambiguity obsolete, continued clarification-seeking is a mismatch to the current task state.\n- Check whether the assistant solved a **different problem model** than the user finally confirmed. Internal arithmetic can be correct while the global framing is wrong.\n- Distinguish derived facts from the **actual target asked**. A correct intermediate quantity is not aligned if the user asked for a different final quantity, output, or state.\n- When multiple answers appear across turns, treat that as a strong signal of **answer drift** or unstable framing.\n- If a later user message changes the implied total, base, target variable, grouping, or relation, expect a **full recomputation**, not a patch to the earlier partial result.\n- When a later user turn supplies the missing final operation, reclassify earlier \u201ccomplete\u201d responses as partial or premature, not fully correct.\n\n### What to preserve vs. what to discard\n- In \u201cWhat Looks Right,\u201d keep only reasoning and relationships that still hold under the **full accumulated user constraints**. Retain valid intermediate results when they remain useful, but do not elevate them to the final answer if the target has changed.\n- In \u201cWhat Needs to Change,\u201d tie each issue to the exact user evidence that invalidates the assistant\u2019s step. Explain why the step was unjustified under the final spec, not merely that it was incomplete.\n- If prior assistant reasoning is mostly aligned, keep it as a trusted anchor and flag only the **delta introduced by the newest user turn**.\n- Preserve only validated reasoning when compacting context; drop obsolete uncertainty, speculative branches, and invented scaffolding. If the assistant\u2019s prior path depended on a user-unsupplied model or assumption, mark it for removal.\n- In edit-mode or append-mode settings, preserve correct subresults and remove any anchoring final conclusion that was premature under the expanded spec.\n\n### Common pitfalls to watch for\n- Do not let earlier assistant reasoning anchor the evaluation if it was based on partial data. It should not survive unchanged once the user adds a decisive constraint.\n- Do not confuse \u201cthe assistant had a plausible guess\u201d with \u201cthe user required that guess.\u201d Only user-grounded facts belong in the task spec.\n- Do not preserve stale uncertainty after the user has completed the specification. A later message can turn an underdetermined problem into a determinate one.\n- Watch for assistant-added structure that the user never supplied: hidden timing assumptions, invented categories, alternative formulas, or other model changes.\n- Do not treat user repetition or incremental detail as noise; these often refine the task, resolve ambiguity, or complete a missing condition.\n- Avoid overcomplicating the task with competing interpretations once the user\u2019s latest phrasing makes one reading clearly dominant.\n- Do not focus only on whether the assistant used a generally valid method; focus on whether it matches the **latest accumulated user intent** and final specification.\n- When the user provides a concrete label, grouping, or average/aggregate description, treat that wording as authoritative rather than decomposing it into an invented lower-level model unless the user explicitly requires that.\n\n### Response-shaping priorities for the analyzer\n- The final judgment should reflect the **current task state after all user shards**, not any earlier snapshot.\n- Explicitly note when a later user message changes the implied total, base, target variable, grouping, or relation, because that is often the precise point where alignment breaks.\n- When the conversation contains enough information for a final answer, expect a **completed response**, not more questions.\n- Treat \u201cWhat Looks Right\u201d as **scoped alignment**, not blanket endorsement: identify which parts remain valid under the expanded spec and which must be revised.\n- Ensure \u201cWhat Needs to Change\u201d names the **last missing operation or newly invalidated assumption** so the next turn can continue from the true task state.",
  "version": 3,
  "history": [
    "",
    "<updated_cheatsheet>\n### Sequencing: how to analyze evolving conversations\n- Start by building a **running constraint set from user messages only**. After every new user turn, re-evaluate the entire task spec rather than appending to an old conclusion.\n- Treat later user turns as **highest-priority revisions** to the spec, not just extra details. If a new shard changes totals, categories, or state transitions, invalidate earlier inferences that depended on the older interpretation.\n- Wait to finalize task understanding until all user-stated constraints are collected. A conversation may look underspecified early on but become fully solvable once later shards arrive.\n- When the user introduces an aggregate, total, remainder, or final leftover after earlier partial amounts, **reconstruct the current state before reasoning backward**. Many failures come from confusing an intermediate value with the final state.\n- If the latest user message completes a missing partition or relation, switch from \u201cneeds clarification\u201d thinking to full system evaluation. Do not keep stale uncertainty after the spec becomes complete.\n\n### State tracking: what to compare across turns\n- Track quantities as a **state-transition chain**: identify what is initial, what is intermediate, and what is current at each turn. This is especially important when the same object changes state multiple times.\n- Check whether each new disclosure refers to the **same entity in a later state** or to a different subset/category. The analyzer should propagate the state change through the spec instead of treating each mention independently.\n- When later turns reveal a remaining amount plus an amount already kept/used/set aside, combine those into the total at that stage before solving any earlier unknowns.\n- For staged percentages, costs, or quantities, verify whether each value applies to the **original total** or to a **remainder after prior deductions**. Misreading the base is a common source of false alignment.\n- After a new shard arrives, compare the assistant\u2019s response against the **full accumulated spec**, not just the latest message. A mathematically valid intermediate result can still be wrong as the final answer.\n\n### Diagnostic patterns: what signals an error\n- Flag any assistant inference that fills a gap the user has not actually filled. This is especially important when the answer depends on a category split, remainder, hidden total, or other unstated partition.\n- If the assistant proposes a value using an unsupported assumption, mark it as wrong if later user turns make that assumption unnecessary, incomplete, or false.\n- Distinguish between **derived sub-results** and the actual requested target. A correct intermediate fact is not aligned if the user asked for a different quantity, final state, or output variable.\n- Separate arithmetic correctness from specification alignment. The assistant may compute correctly from partial data while still failing the user\u2019s final intent.\n- If the assistant asks for more information, check whether later user turns already supply that information implicitly or explicitly. Earlier \u201cneed clarification\u201d reasoning becomes obsolete once the conversation resolves it.\n- When the user adds a total/sum constraint after earlier relational clues, this usually means the analyzer should look for a **system-wide completion**, not continue treating the problem as underdetermined.\n\n### What to preserve vs. what to discard\n- In \u201cWhat Looks Right,\u201d keep only calculations and relationships that remain valid under the **full accumulated user constraints**. Discard partial conclusions that depended on an incomplete terminal condition.\n- In \u201cWhat Needs to Change,\u201d tie the flaw to the exact user evidence that was missing at the time. The corrective note should make clear why the assistant\u2019s step was unjustified under the full conversation.\n- When new information resolves an earlier uncertainty, mark prior \u201cneeds clarification\u201d reasoning as obsolete and note the anchoring risk.\n- If the assistant has already gone down speculative branches, compact the context by removing obsolete uncertainty and irrelevant assumptions so later analysis stays aligned with the actual specification.\n\n### Common pitfalls to watch for\n- Do not let earlier assistant reasoning anchor the analysis summary. If it was based on partial data, it should not survive unchanged once the user adds a decisive constraint.\n- Do not keep stale uncertainty after the user has completed the specification. A later shard can turn an incomplete problem into a fully determined one.\n- Watch for assistant-added assumptions that were never in the user messages, especially extra categories, fees, charge types, alternative formulas, or other invented structure.\n- Do not treat user repetition as noise. Repeated or incremental user messages often refine the task, resolve ambiguity, or supply the missing piece needed to solve it.\n- Avoid overcomplicating the task with an unnecessary template when the accumulated user constraints support a simpler consistent interpretation.\n- Do not focus on whether the assistant used a generally valid method; focus on whether it matches the **evolving user intent** and the final accumulated specification.\n\n### Response-shaping priorities for the analyzer\n- The final judgment should reflect the **current task state after all user shards**, not the state visible at any earlier turn.\n- The analyzer should explicitly note when a later user message changes the implied total, base, or target variable, because that is often the exact point where alignment breaks.\n- When the conversation history contains enough information to determine the answer, the analyzer should expect a final result rather than more questions.\n- For context-edit or compacted summaries, preserve validated relationships, the latest decisive constraint, and the actual target; remove obsolete totals, stale assumptions, and intermediate conclusions that no longer hold.\n</updated_cheatsheet>",
    "### Sequencing: how to analyze evolving conversations\n- Build the task spec **only from user messages**, and update it after every new user turn. Do not let assistant guesses, clarifying questions, or speculative branches become part of the requirement.\n- Treat each later user message as a **constraint revision or completion**, not just extra context. Re-read earlier turns against the latest wording and invalidate any prior interpretation that no longer fits.\n- Before judging the assistant, reconstruct the **full accumulated user spec** and then compare the assistant\u2019s latest turn against that complete state. Avoid stale judgments based on an earlier partial spec.\n- Distinguish \u201c**under-specified so far**\u201d from \u201c**fully specified now**.\u201d Once a later shard supplies the missing terminal condition or relation, stop carrying forward earlier uncertainty.\n- When the conversation is sharded, identify the **minimal complete set of user constraints** needed for a unique answer, and check whether the latest shard completes that set.\n- If a later user turn changes the implied total, base, target, or relation, treat that as the new anchor for reconciliation rather than an optional detail.\n- After every new user message, re-check earlier objections and retire any issue that the new shard resolves. Separate \u201cinsufficient information at the time\u201d from \u201cinsufficient information now.\u201d\n\n### State tracking: how to follow changes over time\n- Model the conversation as a **running state machine**: track what is known, what changed, and what remains unresolved at each turn.\n- When the same entity appears in later turns, determine whether it refers to the **same object in a later state** or to a different subset/category. Propagate state changes instead of treating mentions independently.\n- For partial amounts, remainders, totals, and leftovers, reconstruct the **current state first** before reasoning backward. A value that was intermediate earlier may become the final state after later turns.\n- For rates, proportions, or \u201cmatches in X time\u201d phrasing, verify the **direction of the relation** and the base it applies to. Do not silently convert one kind of quantity into another.\n- When the user later provides a formula or explicit relationship, use it **literally** as the governing rule and recompute from the accumulated facts.\n- In any evolving calculation, separate **intermediate correctness** from **final-state correctness**. A mathematically valid subresult can still be misaligned if the latest user update was not absorbed.\n\n### Diagnostic patterns: what signals an error\n- Flag any assistant step that fills a gap the user has **not actually filled**. Unsupported assumptions are errors even if they produce plausible numbers.\n- If the assistant keeps asking for information after the user has already provided it, mark that as **stale-analysis divergence** rather than caution.\n- If later user turns make an earlier uncertainty obsolete, the assistant should stop acting as if the problem is still ambiguous; continued clarification-seeking is a mismatch to the current task state.\n- Check whether the assistant solved a **different problem model** than the one the user finally confirmed. Internal arithmetic can be correct while the global framing is wrong.\n- Distinguish derived facts from the **actual target asked**. A correct intermediate quantity is not aligned if the user asked for a different final quantity, state, or output.\n- When multiple numeric answers appear across turns, treat that as a strong signal of **answer drift** or unstable framing.\n- If a later total, sum, or remainder appears after earlier relational clues, expect a **full recomputation**, not a patch to the earlier partial result.\n- When the assistant reaches a conclusion under an assumption that the user never stated, mark it as contradiction if later user text shows the assumption was unnecessary, incomplete, or false.\n\n### What to preserve vs. what to discard\n- In \u201cWhat Looks Right,\u201d keep only calculations and relationships that still hold under the **full accumulated user constraints**. Retain valid intermediate results when they remain useful, but do not elevate them to the final answer if the target has changed.\n- In \u201cWhat Needs to Change,\u201d tie each issue to the exact user evidence that invalidates the assistant\u2019s step. The correction should explain why the step was unjustified under the final spec, not merely say it was incomplete.\n- When a new shard resolves ambiguity, compact the context by **dropping obsolete uncertainty and speculative branches**. Preserve the validated path and the latest decisive constraint.\n- If prior assistant reasoning is mostly aligned, keep it as a trusted anchor and flag only the **delta introduced by the newest user turn**.\n- Prefer a single reconciled final specification over competing partial interpretations. Remove earlier branches once the user has effectively chosen one.\n\n### Common pitfalls to watch for\n- Do not let earlier assistant reasoning anchor the evaluation if it was based on partial data. It should not survive unchanged once the user adds a decisive constraint.\n- Do not confuse \u201cthe assistant had a plausible guess\u201d with \u201cthe user required that guess.\u201d Only user-grounded facts belong in the task spec.\n- Do not preserve stale uncertainty after the user has completed the specification. A later message can turn an underdetermined problem into a determinate one.\n- Watch for assistant-added structure that the user never supplied: hidden timing assumptions, invented categories, alternative formulas, or other model changes.\n- Do not treat user repetition or incremental detail as noise; these often refine the task, resolve ambiguity, or complete the missing condition.\n- Avoid overcomplicating the task with competing interpretations once the user\u2019s latest phrasing makes one reading clearly dominant.\n- Do not focus only on whether the assistant used a generally valid method; focus on whether it matches the **latest accumulated user intent** and final specification.\n\n### Response-shaping priorities for the analyzer\n- The final judgment should reflect the **current task state after all user shards**, not any earlier snapshot.\n- Explicitly note when a later user message changes the implied total, base, target variable, or relation, because that is often the precise point where alignment breaks.\n- When the conversation contains enough information for a final answer, expect a **completed response**, not more questions.\n- In compacted summaries or edit-mode outputs, preserve validated relationships, the latest decisive constraint, and the true target; remove obsolete totals, stale assumptions, and resolved uncertainty."
